    }

    @media (max-width: 700px) {
      .header-inner, main, .footer-inner { width: calc(100% - 2rem); }
      .header-inner {
        align-items: flex-start;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 1rem;
        padding-block: 1rem;
      }
      .header-actions { width: 100%; justify-content: space-between; }
      nav { gap: 1.2rem; }
      main { padding-top: 1.3rem; }
      .hero { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1.4rem; padding: 1.8rem 0 2.2rem; }
      h1 { font-size: clamp(3.1rem, 16vw, 5.2rem); }
      .section-heading { align-items: flex-start; flex-direction: column; gap: .7rem; margin-top: 2.4rem; }
      .team-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
      .study { min-height: 0; }
      .future-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
      .debate { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="header-inner">
      <div class="brand">
        <div class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true">&lt;/&gt;</div>
        <div>
          <p class="brand-name">Build &amp; Beyond</p>
          <span class="brand-kicker">Paderborn · Computer Science</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="header-actions">
        <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
          <a href="#team">The team</a>
          <a href="#future-of-se">Our outlook</a>
        </nav>
        <button id="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode"><span aria-hidden="true">☾</span></button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="page-title">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Four perspectives, one shared curiosity</p>
        <h1 id="page-title">Build <span>&amp; beyond.</span></h1>
        <p class="hero-copy">We’re computer science students exploring what thoughtful software can do—and how AI is changing the way we make it. Meet the people, ideas, and questions behind our work.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="hero-note">
        <strong>Our starting point</strong>
        <p>Useful software takes more than fast code: it takes curiosity, good judgement, and care for the systems—and people—on the other side.</p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section id="team" aria-labelledby="team-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Meet the people</p>
          <h2 id="team-title">Different paths.<br>Shared momentum.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>From systems and security to AI and full-stack building, our experiences shape the questions we bring to software engineering.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="team-grid">
        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:akshay.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Akshay">
          <h3>Akshay</h3>
          <p class="study">MSc Computer Science</p>
          <p class="bio">Akshay Pradeep Kumar studies computer science at master’s level. Running and travelling keep him curious beyond his studies, and he has recently started to love cooking. He sees AI as a way for developers to complete tasks faster and believes people may reach new heights in technology with its help. For him, the next era of software engineering will reward creativity in deciding what to make, not just the ability to code it.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Akshay has recently started to love cooking.</em></p>
          <div class="detail-block">
            <strong>Interests</strong>
            <p>Running · Travelling · Cooking</p>
          </div>
          <div class="detail-block">
            <strong>Looking ahead</strong>
            <p>He expects software engineering to become more about creative problem selection and making than coding alone.</p>
          </div>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 We’re teaching machines to code so we can finally spend our time arguing about what to build.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:leman.png}}" alt="Photo of Leman">
          <h3>Leman</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 5th semester, Universität Paderborn · B.Sc., Ahsanullah University of Science and Technology, Bangladesh</p>
          <p class="bio">Leman is a fifth-semester M.Sc. student at Universität Paderborn and earned a B.Sc. from Ahsanullah University of Science and Technology in Bangladesh. Data science, video games, and books are among Leman’s interests. People often describe Leman as a bit introverted—a description Leman disagrees with. Leman sees AI as helpful for quickly handling menial setup and repetitive tasks, while also recognising its effects on the job sector.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: People say Leman is a bit introverted, but Leman doesn’t agree.</em></p>
          <div class="detail-block">
            <strong>Interests</strong>
            <p>Data science · Video gaming · Reading books</p>
          </div>
          <div class="detail-block">
            <strong>Looking ahead</strong>
            <p>Leman expects a shift from line-by-line coding toward system design. AI will take on boilerplate and more of the path from building an app to shipping it; people who can guide it across whole systems will be ahead.</p>
          </div>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI won’t replace good software developers. Better developers are who that can use AI tools better.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:mazhar.png}}" alt="Photo of Mazhar">
          <h3>Mazhar</h3>
          <p class="study">1st-semester M.Sc. Computer Science, Paderborn University · B.Sc. Computer Science and Engineering, Leading University, Bangladesh</p>
          <p class="bio">Mazhar is a first-semester M.Sc. Computer Science student at Paderborn University, with a B.Sc. in Computer Science and Engineering from Leading University in Bangladesh and three and a half years of professional experience. Mazhar enjoys building products, automating repetitive work, and learning about architecture, networking, and security. Mazhar sees AI making routine coding cheaper, increasing the importance of reliable, secure systems and complex engineering judgement.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: After 3½ years in software, Mazhar is back at university learning to delegate to AI—and hoping it still leaves a job.</em></p>
          <div class="detail-block">
            <strong>Interests</strong>
            <p>Building products · Automation · Architecture, fundamentals, networking and security · Exchanging ideas</p>
          </div>
          <div class="detail-block">
            <strong>Looking ahead</strong>
            <p>Mazhar is uncertain what the next 5–10 years will bring and worries progress could outpace safe adoption. The hope is that guidance, security, and responsible use get as much attention as capability—and that people’s roles are considered as work changes.</p>
          </div>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI puts building within more people’s reach; curiosity, creativity and learning how to guide it make the difference.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:sachin.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Sachin">
          <h3>Sachin</h3>
          <p class="study">1st-semester M.Sc. Computer Science, University of Paderborn · B.E. Computer Science and Engineering, DSCE, Bangalore, India</p>
          <p class="bio">Sachin is a first-semester M.Sc. Computer Science student at the University of Paderborn and holds a B.E. in Computer Science and Engineering from DSCE in Bangalore. Exploring AI and full-stack development, fitness, cooking, and competitive gaming all feature in Sachin’s interests. Sachin values AI for boilerplate setup and standard test generation, but warns that generated code can hide logic vulnerabilities. To Sachin, AI amplifies engineers who know how to judge it—and can trap beginners who trust it blindly.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Sachin once spent three hours debugging a production error caused by misspelling his own first name in a configuration string.</em></p>
          <div class="detail-block">
            <strong>Interests</strong>
            <p>AI · Full-stack development · Fitness · Cooking · Competitive gaming</p>
          </div>
          <div class="detail-block">
            <strong>Looking ahead</strong>
            <p>Sachin expects raw code-writing to become a legacy workflow. The engineer’s role will centre on precise specifications, prompt boundaries, and continuous validation: architects and judges, not typists.</p>
          </div>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI shouldn’t replace our critical thinking; it should augment it. Outsourcing our logic to algorithms breeds intellectual laziness.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="future-of-se" class="future-section" aria-labelledby="future-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Our shared outlook</p>
          <h2 id="future-title">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="future-intro">Our routes into computer science differ, but we agree the work is becoming bigger than writing code line by line. Coding agents can speed up routine tasks; making the result useful, secure, and dependable still calls for human judgement.</p>

      <div class="future-grid">
        <div class="future-panel">
          <h3>What we agree on</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>AI is useful for repetitive setup, boilerplate, and standard test generation—but its output still needs review.</li>
            <li>As implementation gets faster, clear problem framing, creativity, and system design matter more.</li>
            <li>Security, reliability, and validation must be part of the process, not afterthoughts.</li>
            <li>People who understand the fundamentals can guide AI tools more effectively and spot where they go wrong.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="future-panel">
          <h3>Where we see it differently</h3>
          <div class="debate">
            <div class="debate-side">
              <strong>More optimistic</strong>
              <p>AI can help developers move faster and make building software accessible to more people, freeing time for creative work.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="debate-side">
              <strong>More cautious</strong>
              <p>Rapid automation brings uncertainty about jobs, hidden vulnerabilities, and whether responsible use can keep pace with new capability.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="closing-note">What we’re learning as we work with AI tools is that speed is only one measure of progress. We want to get better at specifying what we need, checking what agents produce, and building systems people can trust.</p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <div class="footer-inner">
      <p>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. · 2025 · Build &amp; Beyond</p>
    </div>
  </footer>

  <script>
    (function () {
      var root = document.documentElement;
      var toggle = document.getElementById("theme-toggle");

      function updateToggle() {
        var dark = root.getAttribute("data-theme") === "dark";
        toggle.setAttribute("aria-label", dark ? "Switch to day mode" : "Switch to night mode");
        toggle.innerHTML = '<span aria-hidden="true">' + (dark ? "☀" : "☾") + "</span>";
      }

      updateToggle();
      toggle.addEventListener("click", function () {
        var next = root.getAttribute("data-theme") === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";
        root.setAttribute("data-theme", next);
        try { localStorage.setItem("upb-theme", next); } catch (e) {}
        updateToggle();
      });
    }());
  </script>
</body>
</html>
