      <article class="member-card"><img class="member-photo" src="{{image:mazhar.png}}" alt="Photo of Mazhar"><h3>Mazhar</h3><p class="study">1st-semester M.Sc. Computer Science, Paderborn University · B.Sc. Computer Science and Engineering, Leading University, Bangladesh</p><p class="bio">Mazhar is a first-semester M.Sc. Computer Science student at Paderborn University, with a B.Sc. in Computer Science and Engineering from Leading University in Bangladesh and three and a half years of professional experience. Mazhar enjoys building products, automating repetitive work, and learning about architecture, networking, and security. Mazhar sees AI making routine coding cheaper, increasing the importance of reliable, secure systems and complex engineering judgement.</p><p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: After 3½ years in software, Mazhar is back at university learning to delegate to AI—and hoping it still leaves a job.</em></p><div class="detail-block"><strong>Interests</strong><p>Building products · Automation · Architecture, fundamentals, networking and security · Exchanging ideas</p></div><div class="detail-block"><strong>Looking ahead</strong><p>Mazhar is uncertain what the next 5–10 years will bring and worries progress could outpace safe adoption. The hope is that guidance, security, and responsible use get as much attention as capability—and that people’s roles are considered as work changes.</p></div><p class="hot-take">🔥 AI puts building within more people’s reach; curiosity, creativity and learning how to guide it make the difference.</p></article>
      <article class="member-card"><img class="member-photo" src="{{image:sachin.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Sachin"><h3>Sachin</h3><p class="study">1st-semester M.Sc. Computer Science, University of Paderborn · B.E. Computer Science and Engineering, DSCE, Bangalore, India</p><p class="bio">Sachin is a first-semester M.Sc. Computer Science student at the University of Paderborn and holds a B.E. in Computer Science and Engineering from DSCE in Bangalore. Exploring AI and full-stack development, fitness, cooking, and competitive gaming all feature in Sachin’s interests. Sachin values AI for boilerplate setup and standard test generation, but warns that generated code can hide logic vulnerabilities. To Sachin, AI amplifies engineers who know how to judge it—and can trap beginners who trust it blindly.</p><p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Sachin once spent three hours debugging a production error caused by misspelling his own first name in a configuration string.</em></p><div class="detail-block"><strong>Interests</strong><p>AI · Full-stack development · Fitness · Cooking · Competitive gaming</p></div><div class="detail-block"><strong>Looking ahead</strong><p>Sachin expects raw code-writing to become a legacy workflow. The engineer’s role will centre on precise specifications, prompt boundaries, and continuous validation: architects and judges, not typists.</p></div><p class="hot-take">🔥 AI shouldn’t replace our critical thinking; it should augment it. Outsourcing our logic to algorithms breeds intellectual laziness.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="future-of-se" class="future-section" aria-labelledby="future-title">
    <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Our shared outlook</p><h2 id="future-title">The Future of Software Engineering</h2></div></div>
    <p class="future-intro">Our routes into computer science differ, but we agree the work is becoming bigger than writing code line by line. Coding agents can speed up routine tasks; making the result useful, secure, and dependable still calls for human judgement.</p>
    <div class="future-grid"><div class="future-panel"><h3>What we agree on</h3><ul><li>AI is useful for repetitive setup, boilerplate, and standard test generation—but its output still needs review.</li><li>As implementation gets faster, clear problem framing, creativity, and system design matter more.</li><li>Security, reliability, and validation must be part of the process, not afterthoughts.</li><li>People who understand the fundamentals can guide AI tools more effectively and spot where they go wrong.</li></ul></div><div class="future-panel"><h3>Where we see it differently</h3><div class="debate"><div class="debate-side"><strong>More optimistic</strong><p>AI can help developers move faster and make building software accessible to more people, freeing time for creative work.</p></div><div class="debate-side"><strong>More cautious</strong><p>Rapid automation brings uncertainty about jobs, hidden vulnerabilities, and whether responsible use can keep pace with new capability.</p></div></div></div></div>
    <p class="closing-note">What we’re learning as we work with AI tools is that speed is only one measure of progress. We want to get better at specifying what we need, checking what agents produce, and building systems people can trust.</p>
  </section>
</main>
